        <div class=3D"mt-8 main-content">
            <p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7">A histogram is a graph that visuali=
zes the frequency of numerical data. It is commonly used in data science an=
d statistics to have a raw estimate of the distribution of a dataset. Kerne=
l density estimation (KDE) is a method for estimating the probability densi=
ty function (PDF) of a random variable with an unknown distribution using a=
 random sample drawn from that distribution. Hence, it allows us to infer t=
he probability density of a population, based on a finite dataset sampled f=
rom it. KDE is often used in signal processing and data science, as an esse=
ntial tool to estimate the probability density. This article discusses the =
math and intuition behind histograms and KDE and their advantages and limit=
ations. It also demonstrates how KDE can be implemented in Python from scra=
tch. <em>All figures in this article were created by the author.</em></p><p=
 class=3D"leading-8 mt-7"><strong>Probability density function</strong></p>=
<p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7">Let <em>X</em> be a continuous random variable.=
 The probability that <em>X</em> takes a value in the interval [a, b] can b=
e written as</p><div class=3D"mt-7"><img loading=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" cla=
ss=3D"pt-5 m-auto lightense-target" role=3D"presentation" referrerpolicy=3D=
"no-referrer" src=3D"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*7Il5QQmagx=
hM0kOyAzSdlw@2x.png"></div><p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7">where <em>f(x)</em> =
is <em>X</em>'s probability density function (PDF). The <em>cumulative dens=
ity function</em> (CDF) of <em>X</em> is defined as:</p><div class=3D"mt-7"=
><img loading=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" class=3D"pt-5 m-auto lightense-target"=
 role=3D"presentation" referrerpolicy=3D"no-referrer" src=3D"https://miro.m=
edium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*B07NP2eur-r9W66-tfbYvQ@2x.png"></div><p class=
=3D"leading-8 mt-7">Hence the CDF of <em>X</em>, evaluated at <em>x</em>, i=
s the probability that <em>X</em> will take a value less than or equal to <=
em>x</em>. Using Equation 1, we can write:</p><div class=3D"mt-7"><img load=
ing=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" class=3D"pt-5 m-auto lightense-target" role=3D"p=
resentation" referrerpolicy=3D"no-referrer" src=3D"https://miro.medium.com/=
v2/resize:fit:700/1*9B6-UYTlyznrNmDVRR5ccw@2x.png"></div><p class=3D"leadin=
g-8 mt-7">Using the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can show that</p><d=
iv class=3D"mt-7"><img loading=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" class=3D"pt-5 m-auto =
lightense-target" role=3D"presentation" referrerpolicy=3D"no-referrer" src=
=3D"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*x0pZBGwKdiAHX_9mBwrrRg@2x.p=
ng"></div><p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7">which means that the PDF of <em>X</em=
> can be determined by taking the derivative of its CDF with respect to <em=
>x</em>. A histogram is the simplest approach to estimate the PDF of a data=
set, and as we show in the next section it uses Equation 1 for this purpose=
.</p><p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7"><strong>Histograms</strong></p><p class=3D=
"leading-8 mt-7">In Listing 1, we create a bimodal distribution as a mixtur=
e of two normal distributions and draw a random sample of size 1000 from th=
is distribution. Here we mix two normal distributions:</p><div class=3D"mt-=
7"><img loading=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" class=3D"pt-5 m-auto lightense-targe=
t" role=3D"presentation" referrerpolicy=3D"no-referrer" src=3D"https://miro=
.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*lNx3nVJcnTkQabCxqlKCaA@2x.png"></div><p cla=
ss=3D"leading-8 mt-7">Hence, the mean of the normal distributions is 0 and =
4 respectively and their variance is 1 and 0.8 respectively. The mixing coe=
fficients are 0.7 and 0.3, so the PDF of the mixture of these distributions=
 is:</p><div class=3D"mt-7"><img loading=3D"eager" alt=3D"None" class=3D"pt=
-5 m-auto lightense-target" role=3D"presentation" referrerpolicy=3D"no-refe=
rrer" src=3D"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*EjgCG_EZiZ89OkiqZz=
A1wg@2x.png"></div><p class=3D"leading-8 mt-7">Listing 1 plots this PDF and=
 sample in Figure 1.</p><pre class=3D"flex flex-col justify-center border m=
t-7 dark:border-gray-700"><button class=3D"p-1 bg-gray-300 hljs-copy dark:b=
g-zinc-800">Copy</button><code class=3D"p-2 bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-900 ov=
erflow-x-auto language-python hljs" data-highlighted=3D"yes"><span class=3D=
"hljs-comment"># Listing 1</span>

